---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const internal = (segment) => `${base}/${segment.replace(/^\//, '')}`;
---
<Layout
  title="Epistemic Handoff"
  description="Exploratory draft v0.2 on contribution, authority and uptake under cognitive abundance, grounded in contributorship, epistemic dependence and absorptive-capacity traditions."
>
  <header class="page-head paper-head">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Draft paper · v0.2 · 7 September 2026</p>
      <h1>Epistemic Handoff</h1>
      <p class="lede">Contribution, authority and uptake under cognitive abundance.</p>
      <div class="paper-meta" aria-label="Draft status">
        <span>Exploratory</span>
        <span>Machine-assisted</span>
        <span>Lineage reviewed</span>
        <span>Not peer reviewed</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">v0.2 correction</p>
        <h2>The component ideas already have mature intellectual homes.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="large">This draft is now framed as a synthesis and AI-era extension, not as the invention of contributorship, knowledge transfer or uptake.</p>
        <p>Scientific contributorship systems already separate kinds of contribution from a simple author byline. Epistemic-dependence literature explains why knowers rely on other experts. Knowledge-brokering traditions study movement between communities. Cohen and Levinthal's <strong>absorptive capacity</strong> describes the ability to recognize the value of external information, assimilate it and apply it.</p>
        <div class="callout"><p><strong>The question here is narrower: what happens when machine cognition makes it much easier for a low-authority initiator to generate or recognize a promising candidate that they cannot themselves validate?</strong></p></div>
        <p><a href={internal('lineage')}>Read the intellectual-lineage register →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="paper-body">
    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">Abstract</p>
        <p class="paper-abstract">Machine-assisted cognition can lower the expertise required to generate, articulate or encounter sophisticated candidate ideas without equivalently lowering the expertise required to validate them. This draft asks how such candidates can move toward missing expertise without either inflating the initiator's authority or erasing their contribution. It uses <strong>epistemic handoff</strong> as local terminology for that routing problem, while grounding the proposal in established work on contributorship, epistemic dependence, knowledge transfer and absorptive capacity. The draft proposes a claim-sensitive architecture in which standardized contribution roles such as CRediT are recorded separately from the evidence supporting epistemic authority. It then reframes the earlier “Epistemic Uptake Constraint” as an AI-era extension of absorptive-capacity and idea-processing problems: if plausible candidate knowledge becomes dramatically cheaper to produce, the scarce capacity may increasingly be competent recognition, evaluation, validation, integration and realization. The contribution sought here is therefore institutional synthesis and operationalization, not the discovery that knowledge moves between people or that organizations have limited capacity to absorb it.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">1 · Intellectual lineage</p>
        <h2>Contribution, dependence and uptake all predate this draft.</h2>
        <p>Three antecedent traditions are especially important.</p>
        <p><strong>Contributorship.</strong> BMJ's 1990s reforms argued that the author byline poorly represents who actually did what and introduced contributorship and guarantorship. The later ANSI/NISO CRediT taxonomy now supplies fourteen standardized contributor roles. Any system proposed here should use those roles where possible rather than inventing another taxonomy.</p>
        <p><strong>Epistemic dependence.</strong> Hardwig argued that modern knowledge routinely depends on testimony and expertise that an individual knower cannot independently reproduce. Limited personal mastery is therefore not itself a new AI-era condition. What machine systems may change is the scale, speed and opacity with which a person can participate in producing sophisticated objects outside their expertise.</p>
        <p><strong>Absorptive capacity.</strong> Cohen and Levinthal define organizational capacity to recognize valuable external information, assimilate it and apply it. That is strikingly close to the “uptake” problem. The question here is how the relevant capacity behaves when the external supply includes vast amounts of machine-generated candidate knowledge whose surface quality may be difficult to distinguish from its warrant.</p>
        <p>Knowledge brokerage and boundary-spanning literatures supply further antecedents for moving ideas between communities and expertise domains.</p>
        <div class="callout"><p><strong>Epistemic handoff is therefore a proposed arrangement of existing pieces around a new technological pressure, not a claim to have discovered those pieces.</strong></p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">2 · Starting problem</p>
        <h2>“It may be a good idea. I cannot tell.”</h2>
        <p>Cognitive machines can help a person formulate a conjecture, mechanism, proof sketch, design or synthesis that appears promising while the person lacks the domain competence to determine whether it is correct, novel or important.</p>
        <p>Two bad responses are available. One is <strong>authority inflation</strong>: because the initiator possesses a polished artefact, the system treats them as though they possess its expertise. The other is <strong>contribution erasure</strong>: because the initiator lacks credentials or validation capacity, the system discards their role when later experts improve or certify the idea.</p>
        <p>The desired response is more granular: preserve what the initiator actually contributed, identify what remains epistemically missing, and route the candidate toward the functions required to assess it.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">3 · Contribution and authority</p>
        <h2>Use one record for work done and another for warrant.</h2>
        <p>The earlier draft called this the “Principle of Separable Epistemic Roles.” Version 0.2 narrows that claim because contributorship systems already establish that scholarly work comprises different roles.</p>
        <p>The proposed extension is instead <strong>claim-sensitive authority metadata</strong>. CRediT can record Conceptualization, Investigation, Methodology, Formal analysis, Validation, Writing, Software and other contributions. It does not by itself answer who can defend a particular claim or why an audience should rely on that person.</p>
        <div class="status-grid">
          <div><strong>Contribution record</strong><span>Use standardized CRediT roles wherever possible, plus narrow fine-grained descriptors only when needed to preserve initiation, selection or another material event.</span></div>
          <div><strong>Authority record</strong><span>For a claim or claim cluster, record the warrant: demonstrated expertise, expert review, empirical evidence, formal verification, reproduction, or another explicit basis.</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout"><p><strong>Origin deserves credit. Authority requires warrant.</strong></p></div>
        <p>This phrase remains a local operating maxim, not a claim that the general distinction between contribution and responsibility is new.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">4 · The handoff</p>
        <h2>Candidate knowledge needs somewhere to go.</h2>
        <p>An <strong>epistemic handoff</strong> is defined here as a structured transition in which a candidate research object moves toward an actor capable of supplying a missing epistemic function while the earlier contribution record remains intact.</p>
        <p>Examples include:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>a conceptual conjecture handed to a domain expert for novelty and plausibility review;</li>
          <li>a statistical argument handed to a methodologist for model and inference review;</li>
          <li>a proof sketch handed to a mathematician or formal verifier;</li>
          <li>a mechanism handed to an experimental group for empirical testing;</li>
          <li>a validated technical result handed to an engineer or institution capable of realization.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The handoff should specify what capability is missing, what question the receiving steward is being asked to answer, what evidence they add, and which earlier contributions they do not thereby inherit.</p>
        <p>This resembles knowledge brokerage, but the emphasis is deliberately epistemic and claim-sensitive: the handoff is triggered by a known deficit in warrant or competence, not merely by a desire to disseminate information.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">5 · Machine cognition complicates discovery credit</p>
        <h2>The candidate may not have been “the human's idea” in a simple sense.</h2>
        <p>A machine-assisted handoff can involve a more complicated provenance chain. The human may formulate the initial problem and direct a search; the model may generate a candidate; the human may recognize that one output is unusually significant; another model may criticize it; an expert may validate it; and a later team may realize it.</p>
        <p>The draft therefore adopts the Discovery Attribution distinction developed in Working Paper 001:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>candidate generation;</li>
          <li>significance recognition;</li>
          <li>validation;</li>
          <li>integration with prior knowledge;</li>
          <li>realization.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The provenance system should record those events before deciding whether the word “discoverer” belongs to one participant, several, or none.</p>
        <div class="callout"><p><strong>If a machine surfaces an old idea unknown to the participants, the event is rediscovery, not novelty.</strong></p></div>
        <p>This matters to handoff because domain review may reveal not merely that a candidate is wrong but that it is already known. A successful handoff can therefore terminate a novelty claim while still preserving the contribution of independently reaching or usefully reframing the idea.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">6 · Uptake as absorptive capacity under a new load</p>
        <h2>The bottleneck may be receiving candidates, not producing them.</h2>
        <p>Cohen and Levinthal's absorptive capacity already tells us that access to external knowledge does not guarantee the ability to recognize, assimilate or use it. Weitzman's recombinant-growth model similarly highlights the difficulty of processing an enormous space of possible ideas into usable innovations. Simon's attention framework reminds us that abundance of information consumes scarce attention.</p>
        <p>The AI-era extension is a change in load and signal structure. If systems can cheaply generate articulate candidate hypotheses, analyses and designs, institutions may face:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>far more candidates per unit of expert attention;</li>
          <li>weaker correlation between presentation quality and underlying competence;</li>
          <li>more contributions arriving from people outside conventional credential pathways;</li>
          <li>higher value in routing, verification and triage infrastructure;</li>
          <li>greater risk that ranking systems become new bottlenecks or gatekeepers.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The earlier term <strong>Epistemic Uptake Constraint</strong> is therefore retained only as a local shorthand for a research hypothesis about absorptive/evaluative capacity under machine-generated candidate abundance.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">7 · Institutional failure modes</p>
        <h2>Better routing can still build worse gates.</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Credential capture:</strong> already-legible people receive nearly all scarce expert attention.</li>
          <li><strong>Idea appropriation:</strong> validators absorb originator credit when they add authority.</li>
          <li><strong>Flooding:</strong> candidate generation grows faster than evaluation capacity.</li>
          <li><strong>Pay-to-be-heard:</strong> access to verification becomes primarily a function of wealth.</li>
          <li><strong>Authority laundering:</strong> prestige is mistaken for validation of claims never actually examined.</li>
          <li><strong>Filter monopoly:</strong> ranking systems control the visible possibility space.</li>
          <li><strong>False novelty:</strong> machine-assisted rediscovery is routed into publication before prior art is adequately searched.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The desired institution is not unlimited submission. It is accountable routing with interoperable provenance, explicit lineage search, preserved contribution, claim-sensitive warrant and contestable filters.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">8 · Standards-first implementation</p>
        <h2>Do not encode handoff inside a private ontology if established standards can carry it.</h2>
        <p>The current implementation direction is to use <strong>W3C PROV</strong> for entities, activities, agents, derivations and associations; <strong>CRediT</strong> for standardized scholarly contribution roles; and <strong>RO-Crate</strong> for packaging the research object.</p>
        <p>The local extension should be limited to information needed by the handoff problem, such as:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>which claim lacks authority;</li>
          <li>what authority basis currently exists;</li>
          <li>which capability is missing;</li>
          <li>which handoff is requested;</li>
          <li>what validation or correction the handoff returned;</li>
          <li>what novelty classification changed;</li>
          <li>which contribution records must survive the transfer.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>This makes the proposed system an application profile over mature provenance infrastructure rather than another isolated research metadata standard.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">9 · Research questions</p>
        <h2>What remains after the antecedents are taken seriously?</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Does machine-generated candidate abundance measurably reduce the effective absorptive capacity of existing research institutions?</li>
          <li>Can claim-sensitive handoffs improve validation of useful outsider contributions without overwhelming scarce experts?</li>
          <li>How should triage rank a candidate before domain validation without simply rewarding machine polish or credentials?</li>
          <li>Can CRediT + W3C PROV + a small authority extension represent the process adequately?</li>
          <li>What mechanisms preserve originator contribution when validators substantially reformulate a candidate?</li>
          <li>How should validators be rewarded for the many handoffs that correctly terminate as wrong, trivial or already known?</li>
          <li>How should generation, recognition and validation credit interact in machine-assisted discovery?</li>
          <li>What institutional designs keep routing filters contestable?</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">10 · Limitations</p>
        <h2>This remains an institutional hypothesis.</h2>
        <p>No evidence yet shows that the proposed handoff architecture improves scientific output, fairness or expert-attention allocation. The relevant literature review remains broad rather than systematic. Knowledge-brokering, technology-transfer, open-innovation and sociology-of-science literatures require deeper review before any stronger contribution claim.</p>
        <p>The terms “epistemic handoff” and “epistemic uptake” may themselves have closer antecedents than the current search has found. Their usefulness should be judged by whether they support a distinct operational model, not by whether the phrases are locally memorable.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">AI-assistance & contribution declaration</p>
        <h2>How this draft was produced.</h2>
        <p><strong>Human contribution:</strong> the human curator initiated the practical problem that “I have an idea but do not know whether it is worth anything” should trigger competent uptake rather than dismissal; explicitly separated the initiator from those with epistemic authority; directed the subsequent research and required extensive prior-art correction.</p>
        <p><strong>Machine contribution:</strong> OpenAI ChatGPT materially contributed literature search, synthesis, terminology, structuring, criticism and drafting, including the local terms “epistemic handoff” and “epistemic uptake.”</p>
        <p><strong>Novelty and authority:</strong> these contributions do not establish novelty or domain authority. The draft now explicitly relies on and cites antecedent contributorship, epistemic-dependence and absorptive-capacity work.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section references">
